y echo  the darkness in the hallway of the  apartment building didn't seem quite so  dark now  and jack could see the bugs crawling all  over the walls and floor  enormous bugs with hairy legs and  questing antennae  and beady black eyes like obsidian  marbles they became one with the shadows  moving among them and between them the  door of the numberless apartment was  wide open now and inside was more of the  black from outside a slice of it in the  shape of a man-high rectangle  he heard a voice calling him from inside  a voice that seemed made of the darkness  the kind of voice that death would have  if it could talk  it was calling his name and then other  voices joined in until it was a chorus a  cacophony  whispers and screams and seductive purrs  calling him like a million radio  stations all tuned in at once  jack got to his feet and shuffled toward  the open door there was nowhere else to  go  he knew that now ever since he had woken  and seen the dead woman on the bed  he had known that things would have to  end this way his shoulder slumped in  resignation  he crossed the threshold and swallowed  the darkness whole let it swallow him  utterly the room was the same  the pink sheets the white lace curtains  that fluttered in a gentle breeze  he couldn't feel the sun was out and a  square of blue sky  peaked through the lace the shafts of  sunlight falling in stark strips across  the figure of the woman on the bed  the squat fat woman with the perpetual  scowl that was now slack and powerless  jack didn't know how long he'd been  sitting there next to his mother  didn't know how long it had been since  the last breath had left her body  he touched her arm still warm but going  clammy  he choked back a sob he had always lived  here with his mother  since he was a little boy in this  building with the bugs and the yellow  light bulbs that flickered and never  worked on the first floor  he knew no other home was close to no  other person  of course there was a